WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.180
Permissions and ownership shape symbolic-link safety because they determine who can create, replace, read, modify, or remove filesystem entries.

2
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.920
A link-aware design still needs directory permissions that match the trust model.

3
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.240
Least privilege reduces the impact of mistakes.

4
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:22.900
Code that does not need broad filesystem access should not run with it.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.320
Services should have clear filesystem scope, and helper processes should operate with only the access needed for their role.

6
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:33.280
Privileged contexts require extra care.

7
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:41.940
A higher-privilege program may process paths supplied by lower-trust users, configuration, automation, or service inputs.

8
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:49.160
The program should not let a less trusted path string determine access to resources that the path source could not normally reach.

9
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.080
Ownership checks should match the operation.

10
00:00:52.180 --> 00:01:00.740
Reading a file, replacing a file, deleting a file, writing inside a directory, and traversing a tree all have different ownership and permission questions.

11
00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.480
A single check is rarely enough to answer every operation.

12
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.520
Inherited privileges and service accounts should be documented.

13
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:18.660
Reviewers should know which account runs the code, which directories it can modify, which directories are shared, and which paths come from lower-trust sources.

14
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.760
File-handling design is much easier when privilege boundaries are explicit.
